STORY = '''<div class="card" style="border-left-color:#0047BA"><h3>The Cast Net</h3><p class="muted">An original short story for KCE Pathways</p>
<p>(1) The pier at Charlotte Harbor was gray and quiet at six in the morning, and the water below it looked like wrinkled tinfoil. Mateo held the cast net the way Abuelo had shown him: one edge looped over his wrist, a lead weight pinched between his teeth, the rest folded over his arm like a heavy shawl. It smelled like salt and old rope.</p>
<p>(2) "Turn your hips, then let it go," Abuelo said. "The net wants to open. You only have to let it."</p>
<p>(3) Mateo turned and threw. The net flopped into the water like a wet towel, a tangled lump that sank without catching anything. Behind him, two older boys fishing with rods snickered. Mateo's ears burned. He pulled the net up, dripping and knotted, and stared at the boards so he would not have to look at anyone.</p>
<p>(4) "Again," Abuelo said, as if nothing had happened.</p>
<p>(5) The second throw was worse. The third throw opened halfway and folded shut like a fan. By the sixth throw his shoulder ached, and the sun was climbing over the mangroves. "Maybe I'm just not a fisherman," Mateo muttered.</p>
<p>(6) Abuelo sat down on an overturned bucket. "When I was your age in Veracruz, I threw a net every morning for a whole summer before it opened right. My father never helped me. He said the net would teach me if I paid attention." He pointed at the water. "So what is it teaching you?"</p>
<p>(7) Mateo thought about it. Every bad throw had started the same way: he rushed, flinging his arm before his body turned. He took a slow breath. He planted his feet, turned his hips first, and let his arm follow like the end of a whip.</p>
<p>(8) The net spun open into a wide, perfect circle and hit the water with a soft hiss. When he pulled the line, the net came up heavy and silver, flashing with a dozen wriggling pinfish.</p>
<p>(9) Nobody behind him was laughing now. Abuelo just nodded once, the way he did when something was right, and handed Mateo the bucket. "Again," he said. This time Mateo grinned.</p></div>'''

